 800 4173 1133 4358 \nQ 1466 4544 1914 4544 \nQ 2381 4544 2713 4336 \nQ 3046 4128 3228 3744 \nQ 3411 3360 3411 2829 \nz\nM 1920 2144 \nQ 2214 2144 2422 2253 \nQ 2630 2362 2742 2573 \nQ 2854 2784 2854 3085 \nL 2854 3136 \nQ 2854 3437 2742 3648 \nQ 2630 3859 2422 3968 \nQ 2214 4077 1920 4077 \nQ 1626 4077 1418 3968 \nQ 1210 3859 1098 3648 \nQ 986 3437 986 3136 \nL 986 3085 \nQ 986 2784 1098 2573 \nQ 1210 2362 1418 2253 \nQ 1626 2144 1920 2144 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/>\n     </defs>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-43\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-7b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"text_18\">\n    <!-- 3% -->\n    <g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(170.316665 87.155586) scale(0.08 -0.08)\">\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3d\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-7b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"text_19\">\n    <!-- 2% -->\n    <g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(147.541154 108.010759) scale(0.08 -0.08)\">\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3c\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-7b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"text_20\">\n    <!-- 2% -->\n    <g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(147.541154 128.865931) scale(0.08 -0.08)\">\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3c\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-7b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n <defs>\n  <clipPath id=\"pf9bfc691b4\">\n   <rect x=\"98.801562\" y=\"7.2\" width=\"334.8\" height=\"133.056\"/>\n  </clipPath>\n </defs>\n</svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · Sales lost by category at supermarkets near a discounter (other regions)","note":null},"answer":"<p>Losses concentrate in <b>packaged groceries and household goods</b> (13-14%), where products are identical and price wins. Fresh categories barely move (2-3%). <b>So-what:</b> fight on price only for packaged staples, and lean into fresh, where FreshMart is stronger.</p>"},{"q":"Option A: match the discounter's prices on 300 key packaged items in the 30 exposed stores. That gives up 3 margin points on 20% of their sales, and halves the sustained sales loss (8% → 4%). Worth it?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Cost = 30 × $20M × 20% × 3% = <b>$3.6M/year</b>. Benefit = sales loss falls by 4% → 30 × $20M × 4% × 25% = <b>$6.0M/year</b> saved.</p> <p>Net <b>+$2.4M</b> vs doing nothing. Remaining damage ≈ −12 + 6 − 3.6 = <b>−$9.6M</b>. Price matching helps, but it isn't enough on its own.</p>"},{"q":"What else should FreshMart do?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li><b>Private label</b> on packaged staples: discounter-level prices at better margins</li> <li><b>Double down on fresh</b>: bigger produce and deli counters in exposed stores; the discounter's range is thin here</li> <li><b>Loyalty programme</b> with personalised offers to keep high-value families</li> <li><b>Don't</b> cut prices chain-wide: 30 unaffected stores would give away margin for nothing</li></ul>"}],"reco":"Doing nothing puts $12M a year at risk, about a quarter of FreshMart's profit. Respond in the 30 exposed stores only: match prices on ~300 packaged staples (net +$2.4M), launch a private-label range in the same categories, and invest in fresh food, where the discounter is weak and customers don't leave. Avoid a chain-wide price war. Next step: pilot the full package in the first 5 stores the discounter targets, and track weekly sales by category.","great":["Compares every option against doing nothing","Uses the category chart to decide where to fight","Keeps the response limited to the stores that are actually exposed"],"id":7,"slug":"freshmart"}